        ":question/id": "06ab12ca-1261-529b-b77e-4551b3fd2404",
        ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x}$ at the point where $x=1.$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{6}+5x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-12{x}^{5}+5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-12{x}^{5}+5) \\\\  & =(-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{u} \\\\  & =(-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={((-12{x}^{5}+5){e}^{-2{x}^{6}+5x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =(-12(1)^{5}+5){e}^{-2(1)^{6}+5(1)} \\\\  & =-7{e}^{3} \\\\  & =-7{e}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
