        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{3{x}^{2}-3}$ at the point where $x=-1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=3{x}^{2}-3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 6x \\\\  & =6x\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=-1$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-1} & ={6x{e}^{3{x}^{2}-3}|}_{x=-1} \\\\  & =6(-1){e}^{3(-1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$",
