        ":question/problem": "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y={e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}$ at the point where $x=2.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{3}-4x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}-4.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-4){e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={(3{x}^{2}-4){e}^{{x}^{3}-4x}\\,|}_{x=2} \\\\  & =(3(2)^{2}-4){e}^{{2}^{3}-4(2)} \\\\  & =8\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$",
