        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-6{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-6{x}^{2}+6x) \\\\ & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 6 x^{2} + 6 x\\right) e^{- 2 x^{3} + 3 x^{2} - 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=2} & =(-6{x}^{2}+6x)e^{-2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1}|_{x=2} \\\\ & =(-6(2)^{2}+6(2))e^{-2(2)^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1} \\\\ & =-12\\cdot e^{-5} \\\\ & =- \\frac{12}{e^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
