        ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny=e^{u},\\,u=-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=e^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-3{x}^{2}-4x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\ & =e^{u}\\cdot (-3{x}^{2}-4x) \\\\ & =(-3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{u} \\\\ & =\\left(- 3 x^{2} - 4 x\\right) e^{- x^{3} - 2 x^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at $x=-2$, we have to evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|_{x=-2} & =(-3{x}^{2}-4x)e^{-{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+1}|_{x=-2} \\\\ & =(-3(-2)^{2}-4(-2))e^{-(-2)^{3}-2(-2)^{2}+1} \\\\ & =-4\\cdot e^{1} \\\\ & =- 4 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
